DNS (Domain Name System)

What is DNS?

DNS is a system that, in simple terms, converts a domain name into an IP address.

For example, when you enter:

www.tosinso.com

The browser sends a DNS request to a DNS server, and the DNS server finds the IP address for that domain.

DNS records contain information about services such as mail servers and web servers.

FQDN (Fully Qualified Domain Name)

An FQDN is a complete domain name, for example:

www.example.com.

The final dot represents the Root Level Domain.

.com is the Second-Level Domain.

example is the domain name.

www is the sub domain.